refore, equivalent to ${R}^{+}.$\n\nThe other options are *not* equivalent to the interval $(0,\\infty ):$\n\n- The set $R$ includes the negative real numbers.\n\n- The set ${R}_{0}^{+}$ includes the zero.\n\n- The set ${R}^{-}$ does not include the positive real numbers.\n\n- The set ${R}_{0}^{-}$ does not include the positive real numbers." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-183578/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e1e872ac-ef9b-50d5-b74f-3f861cdda8f6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-183578"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-13146"] :question/problem "Which of the following sets is equivalent to $S = \\{2, 4, 8, 2, 4, 2\\}?$ \n\n- $\\{\\{2\\},4,8\\}$\n\n- $\\{2,4,8\\}$\n\n- $\\{2,8\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Two sets are equivalent if they contain the same elements. It doesn't matter if the elements are listed differently or if one set has duplicates and the other does not.\n\nTo determine which of the given sets is equivalent to $S,$ we start by listing the elements of $S,$ ignoring any duplicates:\n\n$$\n2, \\qquad 4, \\qquad 8\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only set equivalent to $S$ is $\\{2,4,8\\}$ since it contains the same elements as $S.$\n\nThe other sets are not equivalent to $S{:}$\n\n- $\\{\\{2\\},4,8\\}$ has the element $\\{2\\}$ that is not in $S.$  Note that $\\{2\\}\\neq 2$.\n\n- $\\{2,8\\}$ is missing the element $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-13287"] :question/problem "Which of the following sets are equivalent to $S = \\{ a, \\{ b \\}, c, \\{ b \\}, a \\}?$\n\n- $\\{ a, \\{b\\}, c \\}$\n\n- $\\{ a, c, b, \\{b\\} \\}$\n\n- $\\{ a, \\{b\\}, c, d \\}$" :question/worked-solution "Two sets are equivalent if they contain the same elements. It doesn't matter if the elements are listed differently or if one set has duplicates and the other does not.\n\nTo determine which of the given sets is equivalent to $S,$ we start by listing the elements of $S,$ ignoring any duplicates:\n\n$$\na, \\qquad \\{b\\}, \\qquad c\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only set equivalent to $S$ is $\\{ a, \\{b\\}, c \\}$ since it contains the same elements as $S.$\n\nThe other sets are not equivalent to $S{:}$\n\n- Set II *not* equivalent to $S.$ It has an element $b$ that is not in $S.$ Note that $\\{b\\} \\neq b.$\n\n- Set III is *not* equivalent to $S.$ It has an element $d$ that is not in $S.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-13286"] :question/problem "Which of the following sets are equivalent to $A = \\{0, 1, 2, 3, \\ldots\\} ?$\n\n- $\\mathbb{N}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{N}_{0}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}^{+}$" :question/worked-solution "The set $A$ is the set of positive integers and zero. It is denoted by $\\mathbb{N}_{0}$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\mathbb{N}_{0}.$\n\nThe other options are *not* equivalent to $A{:}$\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{N}$ does not include the element zero, and $0\\in A.$\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Z}$ includes the elements $-1,-2,-3,\\ldots,$ and $-1 \\not \\in A, -2\\notin A, -3\\notin A, \\ldots\\,.$\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Z}^{+}$ does not include the element zero, and $0\\in A.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
